mand_word":"analyse","difficulty":"higher","total_marks":5,"question_text":"A footballer kicks a ball. During the kick, the leg swings forward and straightens at the knee.\n\nAnalyse the movement at the knee joint as the leg straightens. In your answer, identify:\n- the type of joint\n- the articulating bones\n- the movement\n- the agonist\n- the antagonist\n\n(5 marks)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":5,"points":["Hinge joint","Femur and tibia","Extension","Agonist: quadriceps","Antagonist: hamstrings"],"guidance":"Both bones are needed for the second mark; 'fibula' or 'patella' as one of the two scores 0. 'Flexion' scores 0 for the third mark."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":2,"AO2":3},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":12},{"topic":"Movement Analysis: Levers, Planes & Axes","subtopic":"Mechanical advantage","spec_ref":"1.1.c","command_word":"explain","difficulty":"higher","total_marks":5,"question_text":"A sprinter rises onto the balls of their feet at the start of a race.\n\n(a) Identify the class of lever at the ankle in this movement, and state where the fulcrum, load and effort are. (2 marks)\n\n(b) Explain the mechanical advantage of this class of lever. (2 marks)\n\n(c) Give a sporting example of a third class lever. (1 mark)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":5,"points":["(a) Second class lever","(a) Fulcrum at the ball of the foot, load (body weight) in the middle at the ankle, effort from the gastrocnemius (calf) at the heel","(b) The effort arm is longer than the load arm","(b) So a large load can be moved with a relatively small effort","(c) A valid example, e.g. the elbow during a bicep curl, or the knee when kicking a ball"],"guidance":"In (a), the second mark needs all three positions, or the idea that the load is between the fulcrum and the effort with the effort from the calf muscle."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":2,"AO2":3},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":13},{"topic":"Cardiovascular and Respiratory Systems & Effects of Exercise","subtopic":"The respiratory system and gas exchange","spec_ref":"1.1.d","command_word":"describe","difficulty":"foundation","total_marks":6,"question_text":"This question is about the respiratory system.\n\n(a) Describe the pathway of air from the trachea to the alveoli. (2 marks)\n\n(b) Explain how the alveoli are adapted for gas exchange. (3 marks)\n\n(c) Define tidal volume. (1 mark)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":6,"points":["(a) Trachea → bronchi","(a) → bronchioles → alveoli","(b) The walls are thin (one cell thick), so there is a short diffusion distance","(b) There are many alveoli, giving a large surface area","(b) Each alveolus is surrounded by many capillaries (a good blood supply), which keeps a high concentration gradient","(c) The volume of air breathed in or out in one normal breath"],"guidance":"In (a), the order must be correct. In (b), 'moist lining so gases dissolve' can replace any one of the three adaptations."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":4,"AO2":2},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":14},{"topic":"Cardiovascular and Respiratory Systems & Effects of Exercise","subtopic":"The heart, blood and double circulation","spec_ref":"1.1.d","command_word":"calculate","difficulty":"higher","total_marks":5,"question_text":"This question is about the heart.\n\n(a) During a game of hockey, a player's heart rate is 150 beats per minute and their stroke volume is 120 ml.\n\nCalculate the player's cardiac output. Give your answer in litres per minute. Show your working. (2 marks)\n\n(b) Explain why the wall of the left ventricle is thicker than the wall of the right ventricle. (2 marks)\n\n(c) Give the role of haemoglobin in red blood cells. (1 mark)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":5,"points":["(a) Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume = 150 × 120 = 18 000 ml per minute","(a) 18 litres per minute","(b) The left ventricle pumps blood around the whole body (systemic circulation), the right ventricle only to the lungs","(b) So it must contract with more force to create higher pressure","(c) It carries oxygen (as oxyhaemoglobin) from the lungs to the working muscles"],"guidance":"In (a), 18 000 not converted scores the first mark only. This question asks for working. A correct final answer with no working scores only the mark(s) for the answer itself, not the method marks. Award each method mark when that step is shown. If the working has a slip (e.g. a wrong number copied) but the final answer is correct, withhold only the mark(s) for the step that is wrong."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":3,"AO2":2},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":15},{"topic":"Cardiovascular and Respiratory Systems & Effects of Exercise","subtopic":"Short- and long-term effects of exercise","spec_ref":"1.1.e","command_word":"explain","difficulty":"higher","total_marks":4,"question_text":"Explain two long-term effects of regular aerobic training on the cardiovascular system, and how each one benefits a marathon runner. (4 marks)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":4,"points":["First effect, e.g. cardiac hypertrophy (a bigger, stronger heart) / increased stroke volume / lower resting heart rate (bradycardia) / increased capillarisation / more red blood cells","Benefit of the first effect, e.g. more oxygen is delivered to the working muscles, so the runner can keep working aerobically for longer at a higher intensity","Second, different effect","Benefit of the second effect, e.g. faster removal of carbon dioxide, delayed fatigue, quicker recovery"],"guidance":"Effects on the respiratory or muscular systems score 0. Short-term effects such as increased heart rate score 0. Each benefit must be linked to the effect it follows."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":16},{"topic":"Components of Fitness & Fitness Testing","subtopic":"Applying components of fitness to sport","spec_ref":"1.2.a","command_word":"justify","difficulty":"foundation","total_marks":4,"question_text":"Name the most important component of fitness for each of these performers, and justify each choice.\n\n(a) A 100 m sprinter (2 marks)\n\n(b) A gymnast performing on the beam (2 marks)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":4,"points":["(a) Speed, power or reaction time","(a) A matching justification, e.g. to cover 100 m in the shortest time; to drive out of the blocks; to react quickly to the gun","(b) Balance (or flexibility)","(b) A matching justification, e.g. to stay on the narrow beam during moves and landings without falling; to perform splits and leaps"],"guidance":"The justification mark needs the matching component and must refer to the activity."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":17},{"topic":"Components of Fitness & Fitness Testing","subtopic":"Fitness tests for each component","spec_ref":"1.2.a","command_word":"describe","difficulty":"foundation","total_marks":4,"question_text":"This question is about the multi-stage fitness test.\n\n(a) Which component of fitness does the multi-stage fitness test measure? (1 mark)\n\n(b) Describe how the test is carried out. (2 marks)\n\n(c) Give one limitation of the test. (1 mark)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":4,"points":["(a) Cardiovascular endurance (aerobic endurance)","(b) The performer runs 20 m between two lines (cones) in time with a recorded bleep","(b) The bleeps get faster; the test continues until the performer can no longer keep up, and the level and shuttle reached are recorded","(c) A valid limitation, e.g. it depends on motivation; it is not specific to many sports; results can be affected by the surface or weather"]},"ao_marks":{"AO1":4},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":18},{"topic":"Principles of Training, Optimising Training & Preventing Injury","subtopic":"Types of training","spec_ref":"1.2.b","command_word":"explain","difficulty":"higher","total_marks":4,"question_text":"A footballer wants to improve their fitness.\n\n(a) Describe continuous training and interval training. (2 marks)\n\n(b) Explain why interval training is more suitable than continuous training for a footballer. (2 marks)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":4,"points":["(a) Continuous training: exercising at a steady (moderate) intensity for a sustained period, at least 20 minutes, with no rest","(a) Interval training: periods of work (often high intensity) followed by periods of rest or recovery","(b) A football match involves repeated sprints with periods of recovery (jogging or walking), which interval training copies","(b) So it develops both the aerobic and anaerobic systems / it is specific to the demands of the game"]},"ao_marks":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":19},{"topic":"Principles of Training, Optimising Training & Preventing Injury","subtopic":"Warm up and cool down","spec_ref":"1.2.c","command_word":"describe","difficulty":"foundation","total_marks":3,"question_text":"Describe the three phases of a warm up, giving a suitable example of an activity for each phase for a netball player. (3 marks)","marking_style":"points","scheme":{"max":3,"points":["Pulse raiser (gradually increasing heart rate), e.g. jogging around the court","Stretching or mobility, e.g. dynamic stretches such as lunges or leg swings","Skill-related or sport-specific practice, e.g. passing drills or practising dodges"],"guidance":"Each mark needs the phase with a suitable example. Static stretches are acceptable for the second phase."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":2,"AO2":1},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":20},{"topic":"Principles of Training, Optimising Training & Preventing Injury","subtopic":"Principles of training and FITT","spec_ref":"1.2.b","command_word":"evaluate","difficulty":"higher","total_marks":6,"question_text":"A 16-year-old 1500 m runner is planning a six-week training programme.\n\nExplain how the runner should apply the principles of specificity, progressive overload and reversibility when planning the programme, and evaluate the risks of applying them incorrectly.\n\n(6 marks)","marking_style":"levels","scheme":{"levels":[{"level":3,"marks":[5,6],"descriptor":"Accurate knowledge of all three principles, clearly applied to a 1500 m runner, with a well-developed evaluation of the risks of applying them incorrectly. Ideas are well organised."},{"level":2,"marks":[3,4],"descriptor":"Knowledge of at least two principles, with some application to the runner and some evaluation of the risks."},{"level":1,"marks":[1,2],"descriptor":"Basic knowledge of one or more principles, with little or no application or evaluation."}],"indicative":["Specificity: training should match the demands of 1500 m, e.g. mainly aerobic with some anaerobic work, running-based, using the muscles and energy systems of the race","Progressive overload: gradually increase the training load (e.g. distance, speed, or number of intervals) using FITT, so the body adapts","Reversibility: fitness gains are lost if training stops, e.g. during injury or illness, so the runner should train regularly and keep some activity going","If training is not specific (e.g. mainly weight training or sprinting), the improvements will not transfer well to the race","If overload is increased too quickly, there is a risk of overtraining, injury (e.g. shin splints, stress fractures) and fatigue","If overload is too small or not increased, the runner will plateau and not improve","A young performer is still growing, so overload needs to be particularly carefully controlled","Rest and recovery days are needed for adaptation, but too much rest can lead to reversibility","Varying training can avoid tedium while keeping it specific"],"guidance":"Level 3 needs all three principles applied to the runner and an evaluation of the risks. Definitions alone stay in Level 1."},"ao_marks":{"AO1":2,"AO2":2,"AO3":2},"spag_marks":0,"paper_ref":"ocr-j587-p1-mock1","paper_number":21}]$q$::jsonb) as x(topic text, subtopic text, spec_ref text, command_word text, difficulty text, total_marks int, question_text text, marking_style text, scheme jsonb, ao_marks jsonb, spag_marks int, paper_ref text, paper_number int)
),
ins as (
  insert into public.fm_exam_questions (subtopic_id, topic_id, question_text, command_word, total_marks, difficulty, exam_board, spec_ref, active, paper_ref, paper_number)
  select st.id, t.id, src.question_text, src.command_word, src.total_marks, src.difficulty, 'OCR', src.spec_ref, true, src.paper_ref, src.paper_number
  from src
  join public.subjects s on s.name = 'Physical Education'
  join public.topics t on t.subject_id = s.id and t.name = src.topic
  join public.subtopics st on st.topic_id = t.id and st.name = src.subtopic
  where not exists (select 1 from public.fm_exam_questions e where e.question_text = src.question_text)
  returning id, question_text
)
insert into public.fm_mark_schemes (question_id, marking_style, scheme, ao_marks, spag_marks)
select ins.id, src.marking_style, src.scheme, src.ao_marks, src.spag_marks from ins join src using (question_text);

